.TH GIT-STAGE-BATCH-SUGGEST-FIXUP 1 "August 2026" "git-stage-batch @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-stage-batch-suggest-fixup \- suggest a commit for a fixup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-stage-batch suggest-fixup
[\fB\-\-line\fR \fIIDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-reset\fR|\fB\-\-abort\fR|\fB\-\-last\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
[\fIBOUNDARY\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch fixup suggest
[\fB\-\-line\fR \fIIDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-reset\fR|\fB\-\-abort\fR|\fB\-\-last\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
[\fIBOUNDARY\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B suggest-fixup
materializes the selected hunk or lines as one exact patch without changing
the real index. The patch is based on the frozen HEAD tree; an index-relative
hunk is rejected when that file's index entry differs from HEAD. It searches
each disjoint old-file range independently and commutes the patch backward
through a frozen linear commit range.
.PP
Output reports source-line lineage and the mechanical placement barrier
separately. A placement barrier is candidate evidence, not proof of semantic
ownership. Repeated invocations walk newest-first through commits supported by
either form of evidence.
.PP
Pure additions to an existing tracked file use adjacent old-file lines as
lineage anchors. An insertion into an empty tracked file can be suggested from
placement evidence alone. Whole-file additions are unsupported.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-line, \-\-lines " IDS
Analyze only specific displayed line IDs.
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Start candidate search over from the most recent candidate.
.TP
.B \-\-abort
Clear suggestion state and exit without showing a candidate.
.TP
.B \-\-last
Re-show the last candidate without advancing.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Output versioned JSON containing the Git object format, full object IDs, the
frozen range, exact unit, lineage and placement evidence, candidate sources,
and iteration position. A result without a candidate is still printed as a
complete record and exits nonzero.
.TP
.I BOUNDARY
Commit excluded from the target range. By default, use the fork point, or
merge base, between
.B HEAD
and its configured upstream.
.SH SAFETY
The command does not create commits, move refs, or change the working tree or
real index. The selected patch and history range are bound to stable IDs;
saved iteration state is discarded when the selection, range, or HEAD changes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-stage-batch (1),
.BR git-stage-batch-fixup (1),
.BR git-log (1)
